WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:03.900
HOSTILE TAKEOVERS:&nbsp;

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:17.460
In our modern capitalistic world with&nbsp;
centi-billionaires and multi trillion-dollar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:24.360
mega cap companies, hostile takeovers are a dime&nbsp;
a dozen. Elon Musk just had one last year with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:30.540
Twitter. More times than not though, such hostile&nbsp;
takeovers don’t actually benefit the company being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:36.900
taken over all that much. Sometimes, the acquirer&nbsp;
will dissect and keep the desirable parts of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:42.600
company and viciously shutdown or sell off the&nbsp;
other parts as was the case with Google and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:48.960
Motorola. Google tookover much of Motorola’s&nbsp;
17,000 patents and sold the remaining corpse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:54.180
to Levono. Other times, acquirers will bury&nbsp;
the original company and taint the products&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.180 --> 00:01:00.480
that they run with as was the case with Oracle&nbsp;
and Sun Microsystems/Java. In the 80s and 90s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:08.220
Sun was an IT giant, but today, the brand is&nbsp;
virtually nonexistent because of Oracle. To make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:13.740
things worse, Oracle annihilated the reputation&nbsp;
of Java by trying to sue Google for their use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:20.100
of it. If none of this has transpired after an&nbsp;
acquisition, it may just be a successful one, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:25.620
even that is only in the eyes of the the acquirer&nbsp;
and shareholders. Oftentimes, the original team&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:30.960
who founded the company is completely kicked out&nbsp;
and robbed as was the case with both Instagram&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:36.840
and Whatsapp. Things have gotten so bad between&nbsp;
Whatsapp’s founders and Zuckerberg that Whatsapp’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:42.000
founders are literally advocating for people to&nbsp;
delete Facebook. The original founders aren’t the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:47.400
only victims in such takeovers either. While a big&nbsp;
mighty monopoly might be a dream for investors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:54.240
the same cannot be said about consumers. So,&nbsp;
in almost every case, hostile takeovers end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:59.460
up hurting the original company, the original&nbsp;
product, the founders, and/or the public. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:05.580
this is simply the reality of our modern&nbsp;
capitalistic society. However, there are some rare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:11.100
scenarios in which the brash acquirers get a taste&nbsp;
of their own medicine. Such was the case with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:17.880
Texaco and Getty Oil which was at the time, the&nbsp;
largest takeover in history. This made sense as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:23.820
Texaco was the 6th largest company in the world,&nbsp;
and they were hoping that this acquisition is just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:29.340
what they needed to overtake Exxon and claim&nbsp;
the crown as the world’s largest company. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:35.340
little did they know that this hostile takeover&nbsp;
would not just be a nightmare but straight up be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:41.640
fatal. Just three years later, Texaco would file&nbsp;
for bankruptcy and the brand has been completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:49.260
forgotten about since. So, here’s how one hostile&nbsp;
takeover destroyed the entire empire of Texaco.

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:55.140
A FAMILY FEUD:
Taking a look back, the story of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.140 --> 00:03:00.960
Texaco’s demise can be traced back to the feud of&nbsp;
a completely unrelated family: the Getty family.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:07.020
The Getty family was the owners of Getty Oil which&nbsp;
was at one point one of the largest companies in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:12.960
the world. In fact, it’s visionary founder, Jean&nbsp;
Paul Getty Sr, was regularly listed as the richest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:19.440
man in the world. For example, in 1957, Fortune&nbsp;
listed him as the richest man alive and in 1966,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:24.960
Guinness listed him as the world’s richest&nbsp;
private citizen. Let’s just say, this guy was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:31.560
a billionaire back in the 50s and 60s so he was&nbsp;
really rich, but he didn’t let this wealth get to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:37.260
his head. He understood how volatile his fortune&nbsp;
was and that much of his wealth was just on paper.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:44.160
Jean was a shrewd businessman who was extremely&nbsp;
frugal and obsessed over every deal. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:49.380
he learned Arabic so that he could personally&nbsp;
negotiate deals in the Middle East and not get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:55.140
screwed over by translators. He would also wash&nbsp;
his clothes by hand because he didn’t want to pay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.140 --> 00:04:01.440
anyone else to do it. Unfortunately though, this&nbsp;
mentality didn’t rub off on his family whatsoever.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:07.860
And this would become painfully evident throughout&nbsp;
the 1970s. Getty Oil would take a substantial hit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:15.180
from the 1973 oil crisis, though this was nothing&nbsp;
that Jean couldn’t recover from. Something he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:22.140
couldn’t recover from though was death and this&nbsp;
was his fate in 1976 at the age of 83. Getty Oil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:27.240
was mostly family owned, so you would think that&nbsp;
they would all join forces and try to protect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:32.880
this family empire. But, the reality was that&nbsp;
they were way too busy arguing over who would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:38.160
get control of the fortune. This was the case&nbsp;
with everyone except for Jean’s youngest son,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:44.280
Gordon Getty. Gordon didn’t really care about&nbsp;
the family business. This isn’t to say that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:48.900
wasn’t grateful for his wealth but simply that&nbsp;
he wasn’t looking to run the company or take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:54.600
it to the moon. Instead, he just wanted to write&nbsp;
music and appreciate Opera, and funnily enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.600 --> 00:05:00.600
that’s exactly what he’s been doing to this day.&nbsp;
So, you could say that money and power hadn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:05.580
really corrupted him yet. The rest of Gordon’s&nbsp;
family was astonished by his nonchalance to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:10.560
whole situation, so they figured that he would be&nbsp;
the most level headed person to have control of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:17.460
the company. This was a terrible idea. The reason&nbsp;
Gordon was so nonchalant about the whole situation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:22.920
was because it didn’t concern him. Gordon’s&nbsp;
ideal scenario was selling off the company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:28.620
getting a massive payout, and moving on with his&nbsp;
life. And this is eventually what he would do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:33.480
but we’ll get to that later. To hand over&nbsp;
substantial control over the company to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:36.540
such a guy was simply braindead behavior, but&nbsp;
fortunately, there was one saving grace. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.540 --> 00:05:41.100
family also handed over substantial control&nbsp;
to a trusted family lawyer named C Lansing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.100 --> 00:05:46.980
Hayes Jr. Now, it’s not clear how good of&nbsp;
a businessman this guy was, but atleast he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:52.020
wasn’t hopelessly incompetent. This arrangement&nbsp;
would take the company through the next 6 years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:58.020
but in 1982, C Lansing would also pass away&nbsp;
which meant that his control would role over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.020 --> 00:06:05.940
to Gordon. Gordon now had a 40% stake in Getty&nbsp;
Oil, and let’s just say, he was clueless as ever.

00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:09.960
GORDON TAKES OVER:
Now that Gordon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:14.940
had such a large stake in the company, he&nbsp;
felt that he was obliged to do something,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.940 --> 00:06:19.800
so he started with trying to address&nbsp;
their rockbottom stock price. You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:25.500
the oil that the company owned itself should’ve&nbsp;
given the company a valuation of $100 per share,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:33.180
but the company was somehow trading at just $50&nbsp;
per share. So, Gordon decided that it would be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.180 --> 00:06:38.400
brilliant idea to bring in some external help, but&nbsp;
he would walk straight into the jaws of a shark:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:44.340
T. Boone Pickens. This guy was one of the&nbsp;
most infamous corporate raiders of all time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:51.300
and back in the 1980s, this was the number one guy&nbsp;
you wanted to avoid as a struggling company. Yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:57.000
Gordon would go straight to this guy and divulge&nbsp;
a bunch of company secrets. You know how they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:01.740
say that if you have a good experience with a&nbsp;
car salesman, you probably got scammed? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:07.860
this sentiment couldn’t be truer with Gordon and&nbsp;
T. Boone Pickens. Gordon felt that Pickens gave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:12.240
him really good advice and that it would be a&nbsp;
good idea to setup a meeting with the chairman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:19.080
of Getty, Sidney Peterson. Peterson actually went&nbsp;
into the meeting with an open mind, but he quickly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:23.580
realized that Gordon had already spilled the&nbsp;
beans, and he could see exactly what Pickens was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.580 --> 00:07:27.300
trying to do. So, he would instantly force Pickens&nbsp;
to sign an win winnightmareblunderwin winagreement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.300 --> 00:07:32.040
that he wouldn’t attempt a hostile takeover.&nbsp;
Peterson probably left the meeting with a sigh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.040 --> 00:07:36.780
of relief that he was able to cover for Gordon’s&nbsp;
blunder and stay one step ahead of Pickens.&nbsp;

00:07:36.780 --> 00:07:41.460
But, it didn’t take long for Gordon to&nbsp;
start talking with yet another shark:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:46.800
the Bass Brothers. Fearful that Gordon was gonna&nbsp;
leak company secrets to all of Wall Street,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:52.800
Peterson would bring in external advisors of&nbsp;
his own in 1983: Goldman Sachs. Goldman didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:57.480
know about the distrust between the board and&nbsp;
Gordon, so they would make a recommendation purely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.480 --> 00:08:02.760
based on the financials of the company. Goldman&nbsp;
suggested that Getty buyback $500 million worth of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.760 --> 00:08:07.800
stock. Given that their stock was trading for half&nbsp;
of the value of their oil, this was great advice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.800 --> 00:08:12.180
If they were able to complete this buyback and&nbsp;
follow it up would a strong corporate turnaround,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.180 --> 00:08:18.120
they would simply be even richer. But, there was&nbsp;
one fatal flaw with this plan. If the company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.120 --> 00:08:23.640
followed through with this repurchase plan, Gordon&nbsp;
would end up with over a 50% stake meaning that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.640 --> 00:08:29.460
he could do whatever he wanted without the board.&nbsp;
This would be suicide for the company and this was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.460 --> 00:08:34.980
simply confirmed by Gordon’s incoherent statement&nbsp;
that followed. He said quote, “What I really want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.980 --> 00:08:38.320
is to find the optimum way to optimize value.”&nbsp;
Yeah, there was no way the board was gonna let&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:43.200
this dude takeover. So, they would go ahead and&nbsp;
reject the idea of a stock buyback, but this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:47.940
simply increased the strife between the board and&nbsp;
Gordon. Gordon started to feel that the next step&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.940 --> 00:08:55.260
for the company wasn’t to get external help but to&nbsp;
get rid of the board and this led to all out war.

00:08:55.260 --> 00:08:59.340
TEXACO STEPS IN:&nbsp;

00:08:59.340 --> 00:09:05.220
All Gordon had to do was figure out how to&nbsp;
increase his stake by a mere 10%, and the it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.220 --> 00:09:11.280
didn’t long for him to find the perfect target:&nbsp;
the Getty Museum. The Getty Museum owned 12% of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.280 --> 00:09:16.860
the company and was led by a man named Harold&nbsp;
Williams. If Gordon could get Williams on his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.860 --> 00:09:22.320
side, well then, he could kick out the board, sell&nbsp;
the company, and move on. To winover Williams’&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:27.480
support, Gordon offered to buyout the shares&nbsp;
of the museum at a very favorable price, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.480 --> 00:09:30.714
Williams didn’t want any part of this nonsense.&nbsp;
He knew that this would simply lead to years if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.714 --> 00:09:33.840
not decades of corporate drama that he didn’t want&nbsp;
any part of, but he would change his mind after a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.840 --> 00:09:39.060
new development. You see, it wasn’t just Gordon&nbsp;
that was trying to kick out the board. The board&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.060 --> 00:09:44.100
was trying to reduce Gordon’s influence as well,&nbsp;
so they had recruited Gordon’s 15 year old nephew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.100 --> 00:09:47.820
to sue his Gordon into implementing a co-trustee.&nbsp;
This would effectively cut his control by half,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.820 --> 00:09:53.160
but this move would also push Williams over&nbsp;
the edge. He couldn’t believe that the board&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.160 --> 00:09:57.840
was leveraging children to push out Gordon, so&nbsp;
he would agree to help Gordon sell the company.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.840 --> 00:10:03.840
To accomplish this, he would bring in the CEO&nbsp;
of Pennzoil, Hugh Liedtke. Pennzoil wasn’t big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:10.020
enough to buyout the entirety of Getty, but they&nbsp;
could help Gordon get rid of the board. Liedtke&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.020 --> 00:10:14.700
would agree to buy 20% of Getty at $100 per share&nbsp;
which would give him a seat on the board. From&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.700 --> 00:10:20.520
there, he could buy out the museum which would&nbsp;
allow him and Gordon to takeover the company. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.520 --> 00:10:27.000
put the board in a super sticky situation. They&nbsp;
couldn’t outright refuse or accept this offer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:32.460
This offer was substantially higher that the stock&nbsp;
was trading for. So, if they rejected the offer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.460 --> 00:10:37.380
they would open themselves up to shareholder&nbsp;
lawsuits. At the same time though, Goldman valued&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.380 --> 00:10:43.500
the company at $120 per share, so if they accepted&nbsp;
the offer, they would still open themselves up to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.500 --> 00:10:49.260
shareholder lawsuits. The board countered that&nbsp;
they would do the deal for $120, but Liedtke&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.260 --> 00:10:55.740
was only willing to go up to $112.50. At this&nbsp;
point, the board could’ve put up more resistance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.740 --> 00:11:01.320
but that wouldn’t have accomplish much. If they&nbsp;
turned down Liedtke, Gordon would simply become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.320 --> 00:11:06.420
even more aggressive and bring in someone else.&nbsp;
So, with that, all parties would agree to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.420 --> 00:11:13.080
deal in principle and it seemed like this is how&nbsp;
this multi year family feud would finally end. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.080 --> 00:11:20.820
then, out of nowhere, Texaco would step in. They&nbsp;
said, forget $120. We’ll buy everything for $125.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.820 --> 00:11:26.370
We’ll buy the public share, Gordon’s share, the&nbsp;
museum’s share, everything. This was a win win win&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.370 --> 00:11:30.720
win scenario. The board was not only able to sell&nbsp;
the company for slightly more than fair value,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.720 --> 00:11:35.640
but they could ensure that Gordon wouldn’t get&nbsp;
control of the company either. As for Gordon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.640 --> 00:11:41.700
well he didn’t want the company in the first&nbsp;
place, what he wanted was out, so he was happy to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.700 --> 00:11:46.920
take the money and focus on music. Meanwhile, the&nbsp;
Museum was able to get out of this drama without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.920 --> 00:11:53.580
have to side with the board or Getty. And as for&nbsp;
Texaco, well, they now had a chance at overtaking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.580 --> 00:11:59.760
Exxon and becoming the world’s largest company.&nbsp;
Given that all parties were ecstatic, the deal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.760 --> 00:12:07.380
would go through in no time, but there was one&nbsp;
person who wasn’t happy at all: Hugh Liedtke.

00:12:07.920 --> 00:12:13.740
PENNZOIL GETS REVENGE:
Before Getty completed the deal with Texaco, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.740 --> 00:12:19.560
had already agreed to do the deal with Pennzoil&nbsp;
in principle. Texaco was aware of this, but they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.560 --> 00:12:24.480
assumed that Pennzoil would just let it go. At&nbsp;
the end of the day, they had put up a good fight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.480 --> 00:12:31.020
and they were close, but no cigar. Texaco had come&nbsp;
out on top and it was time for Pennzoil to accept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.020 --> 00:12:35.745
their loss and move on, but Pennzoil had different&nbsp;
plans. Yes, they had lost out on Getty but not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.745 --> 00:12:37.560
because they were incompetent or outsmarted but&nbsp;
because they were cheated. They had already struck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.560 --> 00:12:43.620
a deal with Getty, and it was not only distasteful&nbsp;
for Texaco to step in but a breach of contract,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.620 --> 00:12:50.820
and Pennzoil was gonna get revenge through legal&nbsp;
means. This was a nightmare for Texaco. They had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.820 --> 00:12:57.120
just bet the farm on Getty. They had just spent&nbsp;
$9.9 billion on the acquisition which was again&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.120 --> 00:13:03.060
the largest in history. This was by no means&nbsp;
a small amount for even Texaco themselves. At&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.060 --> 00:13:09.780
the time, they were making about $1.2 billion per&nbsp;
year, so they had basically bet 8 years worth of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.780 --> 00:13:15.780
profit on Getty. And, who knows how much debt&nbsp;
and leverage they had to use to complete this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.780 --> 00:13:22.620
deal. So, Texaco had no doubt made a risky bet&nbsp;
with Getty, but they felt that if they could just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.620 --> 00:13:28.680
turn around the company, they would be rewarded&nbsp;
insanely. This lawsuit, however, threw a wrench&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.680 --> 00:13:35.340
in all of their plans. The last thing they wanted&nbsp;
to do was fight another oil giant or worse, payout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.340 --> 00:13:42.780
billions of dollars to them. Pennzoil nonchalantly&nbsp;
wanted a full $10.53 billion, and given that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.780 --> 00:13:47.820
did have the deal in principle, it was easy&nbsp;
for the jury to side with Pennzoil. For Texaco,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.820 --> 00:13:50.760
this was a death sentence, as they were basically&nbsp;
being forced to buy Getty for a second time. At&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.760 --> 00:13:54.540
least with Getty, they were getting an&nbsp;
income producing entity with assets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.540 --> 00:13:59.940
employees, and reputation. With the lawsuit,&nbsp;
the money was just going straight to Pennzoil’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.940 --> 00:14:04.260
bottomline. Texaco would of course appeal&nbsp;
and the case with go to the supreme court,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.260 --> 00:14:10.620
but the supreme court would simply humiliate&nbsp;
Texaco. The justices didn’t even go as far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.620 --> 00:14:15.900
as discussing whether Texaco should have to&nbsp;
pay. They straight up said that Texaco didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.900 --> 00:14:21.780
even have the right to appeal this decision and&nbsp;
that their claims lacked merit. And with that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.780 --> 00:14:27.720
Texaco had no choice but to pay. And given that&nbsp;
they couldn’t pay, they had no choice but to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.720 --> 00:14:34.140
file for bankruptcy on April 12, 1987. Now, that&nbsp;
Texaco had been completely driven to the ground,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.140 --> 00:14:40.500
Pennzoil would finally ease up. They would agree&nbsp;
to a much lower but still devastating amount of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.500 --> 00:14:46.920
$3 billion. Texaco would eventually emerge from&nbsp;
bankruptcy, but it was never really the same.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.920 --> 00:14:52.200
They would face a slow decline throughout the 90s&nbsp;
before being bought out by Chevron for $35 billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.200 --> 00:14:58.080
in 2000. Over the next decade, Chevron would&nbsp;
slowly convert Texaco gas stations to Chevron&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.080 --> 00:15:05.100
gas stations or completely shut them down. Today,&nbsp;
there’s still 1297 Texaco gas stations across the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.100 --> 00:15:12.240
US which just goes to show how big Texaco really&nbsp;
was. But, while you might be able to spot a Texaco&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.240 --> 00:15:17.220
on occasion, the brand is effectively&nbsp;
dead all because of a hostile takeover.

00:15:17.220 --> 00:15:21.000
TEXACO TAKEAWAY:&nbsp;

00:15:21.000 --> 00:15:26.100
In the end, Texaco messed with something&nbsp;
that they had no business messing with.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.100 --> 00:15:31.020
Getty was a declining brand that was&nbsp;
filled with drama, backstabbing, ego,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:31.020 --> 00:15:36.600
but ironically everyone on the Getty side&nbsp;
of things came out really well. The Texaco&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.600 --> 00:15:42.480
